---
import Legal from "@layouts/Legal.astro";
import { SITE } from "@data/site";
---
<Legal
  title="Terms of Service"
  description="The terms that govern your use of Jaroku."
  intro="These terms are a plain-language reflection of how Jaroku actually works. If any part of them contradicts what the software does, the software is right and this page is a bug — please tell us."
>
  <h2>1. What Jaroku is</h2>
  <p>
    Jaroku is a desktop application for building, deploying and operating LangGraph agents. It is
    distributed as software you install on a computer you control. Nothing about running it requires
    an account with us; certain features (billing, sync, deployment orchestration) do.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Your account</h2>
  <p>
    You may create an account to use paid tiers or team features. You are responsible for keeping your
    credentials secure and for what happens under your account. If you believe your account has been
    accessed by somebody else, email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    We will terminate the account of anybody using Jaroku to violate the law, to generate content that
    is unlawful in the jurisdiction they use it from, or to abuse other users. Where possible we tell
    you first; where we can&rsquo;t, we&rsquo;ll tell you afterwards.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Your content and your agents</h2>
  <p>
    Everything you build in Jaroku belongs to you. Every generated agent is a plain LangGraph project
    that imports nothing from us &mdash; if you cancel your subscription, delete your account, or the
    company disappears, the folder you generated last week still runs on any LangGraph-compatible host.
    That&rsquo;s a promise the software enforces, not one this page invents.
  </p>
  <p>
    Traces, evaluation records, and audit logs are your data. Retention is per-tier and honest about
    what it is: 7 days on Free, 90 days on Pro, 365 days on Team.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Provider keys and inference costs</h2>
  <p>
    On the Free tier you must supply your own provider API key. Inference does not run through us and
    we do not see the requests or the responses. On Pro and Team we route inference through our
    infrastructure at your option, using a monthly credit that&rsquo;s included in the tier price;
    overage settles at cost + 15%. Costs are recorded per-step in the trace and are summed by the app;
    a crashed run still reports what it spent.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Fair use and rate limits</h2>
  <p>
    We do not currently rate-limit the desktop client (which binds to localhost anyway). We do
    rate-limit any account-scoped API we operate &mdash; billing, sync, deployment orchestration
    &mdash; and reserve the right to lower a limit if a specific pattern of use is harming other users
    or costing us more than the tier price. This is a last resort, not a first move.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Deployments to your own infrastructure</h2>
  <p>
    Deployments land in your Railway account. We orchestrate the deploy; we do not host the container.
    If your account is disabled, your provider key is rejected, or your Railway bill goes unpaid, the
    deployment stops working &mdash; and Jaroku will tell you what it saw, not silently retry into
    someone else&rsquo;s infrastructure.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Warranty</h2>
  <p>
    Jaroku is provided <em>as is</em>. To the fullest extent permitted by law, we disclaim any implied
    warranty of merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose, or non-infringement. Anything an
    agent does with a tool it has been granted is your responsibility &mdash; the software helps you
    scope those grants narrowly, and every high-impact tool call stops to ask before it runs, but the
    decision to grant is yours.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Liability</h2>
  <p>
    To the fullest extent permitted by law, our aggregate liability arising from your use of Jaroku is
    capped at the amount you paid us in the twelve months before the claim arose. We are not liable
    for indirect, incidental, special, consequential or exemplary damages, including lost profits,
    even where advised of the possibility.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Changes to these terms</h2>
  <p>
    We may update these terms. When we do, we&rsquo;ll change the &ldquo;current as of&rdquo; date at
    the top, list what changed in the release notes, and give material changes at least 30 days&rsquo;
    notice before they take effect for existing accounts.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Governing law</h2>
  <p>
    These terms are governed by the laws applicable at the maintainer&rsquo;s primary residence, without
    reference to conflict-of-laws principles. This is a small, honest project; if we have a
    disagreement, email first &mdash; almost every one is a misunderstanding that resolves in a paragraph.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about these terms: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>
</Legal>
